Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on the Prevention of and Countermeasures against School Violence and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Publication of Implementation Plans, Etc.)
① Under Article 6(4) of the Act on the Prevention of and Countermeasures against School Violence (hereinafter referred to as the "Act"), the superintendents of education (hereinafter referred to as "superintendents") of the Special Metropolitan City, Metropolitan Cities, Special Self-Governing Cities, Provinces and Special Self-Governing Provinces (hereinafter referred to as "Cities/Provinces") shall publish the following matters concerning City/Province implementation plans for the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence under paragraph (3) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "implementation plans") through the websites of the relevant City/Province offices of education or other means.
1. Performance achieved under the previous year's implementation plan;
2. The following year's implementation plan.
② Where a superintendent changes the content under subparagraph 2 of paragraph ①, the superintendent shall publish the changed content without delay.
③ Performance in the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence by City/Province offices of education under Article 6(6) of the Act shall be evaluated as part of the evaluation of local educational administrative agencies under Article 9(2) of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act, and the results shall be published.

Article 2-2 (Establishment and Operation of Specialized Educational Institutions for Responding to School Violence, Etc.)
① Under Article 6-2(1) of the Act, the Minister of Education may establish and operate specialized educational institutions performing the following duties.
1. Operation of protective facilities for students' healing and recovery;
2. Research, including investigation and analysis, concerning students' healing and recovery;
3. Subject instruction and alternative education for victim students of school violence;
4. Counseling and operation of healing and recovery programs for victim students of school violence;
5. Other matters the Minister of Education considers necessary concerning students' healing, recovery and related matters.
② Details necessary for the operation of specialized educational institutions under paragraph ① shall be determined by the Minister of Education.

Article 2-3 (Designation and Operation of School Violence Prevention Centers, Etc.)
① Under Article 6-2(2) of the Act, the Minister of Education may designate school violence prevention centers from among the following institutions or organizations.
1. Public institutions under Article 4 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
2. Government-funded research institutions under Article 2 of the Act on the Establishment, Operation and Fostering of Government-Funded Research Institutions;
3. Other institutions or organizations recognized by the Minister of Education as having the professional personnel and dedicated organization necessary to perform school violence prevention and response duties.
② An institution or organization wishing to be designated a school violence prevention center under paragraph ① shall satisfy all the following criteria.
1. It shall have the organization and personnel necessary to perform a school violence prevention center's duties;
2. It shall have the offices and facilities necessary to perform a school violence prevention center's duties;
3. It shall have a business plan and operating regulations necessary to perform a school violence prevention center's duties.
③ Details of the criteria for designation of school violence prevention centers under paragraph ② shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education.
④ When intending to designate a school violence prevention center, the Minister of Education shall publicly announce a designation plan containing the criteria under paragraph ② in the Official Gazette or on the Ministry of Education website for at least ten days.
⑤ An institution or organization wishing to be designated a school violence prevention center shall submit an application for designation to the Minister of Education with the following documents.
1. Current holdings or plans for securing the organization and personnel necessary to perform the duties;
2. Current holdings or plans for securing the offices and facilities necessary to perform the duties;
3. A business plan and operating regulations necessary to perform the duties.
⑥ Upon designating a school violence prevention center, the Minister of Education shall post that fact in the Official Gazette or on the Ministry of Education website.
⑦ In addition to matters provided for in paragraphs ① through ⑥, matters necessary for the designation period, procedures and operation of school violence prevention centers shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education. <Amended 2025.5.20>

Article 3 (Operation of the Committee on Countermeasures against School Violence)
① The chairperson of the Committee on Countermeasures against School Violence under Article 7 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Countermeasures Committee") shall convene and preside over its meetings.
② A Countermeasures Committee meeting shall be convened once every half-year; provided that meetings may be convened as needed at the request of at least one-third of the members in office or where the chairperson deems it necessary.
③ When convening a meeting, the chairperson shall inform each member of the date, time, place and agenda at least five days before the meeting; provided that this shall not apply where an urgent meeting must be convened.
④ A meeting of the Countermeasures Committee shall open with a majority of its members in office present and adopt resolutions with the approval of a majority of members present.
⑤ Where necessary, the chairperson may have experts and other persons attend a meeting and speak on the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence.
⑥ Members, experts and other persons attending meetings may be paid allowances and travel expenses within budgetary limits; provided that this shall not apply where a member who is a public official attends a meeting directly related to the member's official duties.

Article 3-2 (Dismissal of Members of the Countermeasures Committee)
The President may dismiss a member of the Countermeasures Committee under subparagraphs 2 through 8 of Article 8(3) of the Act in any of the following cases.
1. The member becomes unable to perform duties because of a physical or mental impairment;
2. The member engages in misconduct related to the member's duties;
3. The member is deemed unsuitable because of neglect of duties, damage to dignity or other grounds;
4. The member expresses difficulty in performing duties.

Article 4 (Composition and Operation of the Working Committee on Countermeasures against School Violence)
① The Working Committee on Countermeasures against School Violence under Article 8(6) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Working Committee") shall consist of no more than 12 members, including one chairperson (hereinafter referred to as the "Working Committee Chairperson"). <Amended 2013.3.23>
② The Vice Minister of Education shall serve as Working Committee Chairperson. Members shall consist of one person each designated by the head of the relevant institution from among members of the Senior Civil Service of the Ministry of Finance and Economy, Ministry of Science and ICT, Ministry of Education, Ministry of Justice, Ministry of the Interior and Safety, Ministry of Culture, Sports and Tourism, Ministry of Health and Welfare, Ministry of Gender Equality and Family, Office for Government Policy Coordination and Broadcasting, Media and Communications Commission, and police officials of the rank of senior superintendent general or superintendent general at the Korean National Police Agency. <Amended 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26, 2025.10.1, 2025.12.30>
③ A person who designated a Working Committee member under paragraph ② may withdraw the designation where the member falls under any subparagraph of Article 3-2. <Added 2016.5.10>
④ There shall be one executive secretary to handle the affairs of the Working Committee, designated by the Working Committee Chairperson from among public officials of the Ministry of Education. <Amended 2013.3.23, 2016.5.10>
⑤ Where the Working Committee Chairperson cannot perform duties for unavoidable reasons, a member designated in advance by that chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf. <Amended 2016.5.10>
⑥ Meetings shall be convened before Countermeasures Committee meetings or where the Working Committee Chairperson deems it necessary. <Amended 2016.5.10>
⑦ Where necessary to examine and support deliberation on matters to be submitted to the Countermeasures Committee and perform other duties, the Working Committee may have interested parties or relevant experts attend to give opinions, or request submission of opinions. <Amended 2016.5.10>
⑧ When convening a meeting, the Working Committee Chairperson shall inform each member of its date, time, place and agenda at least seven days before the meeting; provided that this shall not apply where an urgent meeting must be convened. <Amended 2016.5.10>

Article 5 (Composition and Operation of Regional Committees on Countermeasures against School Violence)
① The deputy head of the City/Province (meaning the deputy mayor or deputy governor for administrative affairs under Article 71(2) of the Enforcement Decree of the Local Autonomy Act; where a local government has two such deputy mayors or deputy governors, the first deputy mayor or first deputy governor for administrative affairs) shall chair the Regional Committee on Countermeasures against School Violence under Article 9(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Regional Committee"). <Amended 2025.5.20>
② The chairperson of a Regional Committee shall convene and preside over its meetings.
③ Where the chairperson cannot perform duties for unavoidable reasons, a member designated in advance by that chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf.
④ Regional Committee members shall be appointed or commissioned by the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Provincial Governor or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "City/Province Governor") in consultation with the superintendent, from among persons with extensive knowledge and experience and a strong commitment to youth protection who fall under any of the following subparagraphs. <Amended 2020.2.25, 2020.12.31>
1. The bureau director responsible for youth protection in the relevant City/Province and the bureau director responsible for student guidance in the City/Province office of education;
2. A member of the relevant City/Province council or education committee;
3. A police official belonging to the relevant City/Province Police Agency;
4. A teacher with at least five years of student guidance experience;
5. A judge, prosecutor or attorney-at-law;
6. A person who holds or has held a position of at least assistant professor at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act or an equivalent position at a youth-related research institution, and has expertise in school violence issues;
7. A person who has specialized in youth protection activities for at least five years at a youth guidance and protection organization;
8. A parent who serves or has served as a member of a school steering committee under Article 31(1) of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act (hereinafter referred to as a "school steering committee") or a Committee for Deliberation on Countermeasures against School Violence under Article 12(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "Deliberation Committee");
9. Other persons with knowledge and experience in school violence prevention and youth protection.
⑤ Regional Committee members shall serve a term of two years; provided that a member newly commissioned because of a member's resignation or similar circumstances shall serve the remainder of the predecessor's term.
⑥ A City/Province Governor may dismiss or discharge a Regional Committee member under subparagraphs 2 through 9 of paragraph ④ where the member falls under any subparagraph of Article 3-2. <Added 2016.5.10>
⑦ There shall be one executive secretary to handle Regional Committee affairs, selected jointly by its chairperson and the superintendent from among officials of the City/Province or City/Province office of education. <Amended 2016.5.10>
⑧ Article 3(2) through (6) shall apply mutatis mutandis to the operation of Regional Committee meetings. In such cases, "Countermeasures Committee" shall be construed as "Regional Committee". <Amended 2016.5.10>

Article 6 (Composition and Operation of Regional Working Committees on Countermeasures against School Violence)
A working committee under Article 9(2) of the Act shall consist of no more than seven practitioners and private sector experts in the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence.

Article 6-2 (Submission of School Violence Preventive Measures)
① Under Article 10(2) of the Act, a Regional Committee shall submit the following matters concerning regional school violence preventive measures under paragraph (1) of that Article (hereinafter referred to as "preventive measures") to the Minister of Education within four months after the start of the following year's school year.
1. Performance achieved under the previous year's preventive measures;
2. The following year's preventive measures.
② Where a Regional Committee changes the content under subparagraph 2 of paragraph ①, it shall submit the changed content to the Minister of Education without delay.

Article 7 (Composition and Operation of Regional Consultative Councils on Countermeasures against School Violence)
① The deputy head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall chair the Regional Consultative Council on Countermeasures against School Violence under Article 10-2 of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Regional Consultative Council").
② The chairperson of a Regional Consultative Council shall convene and preside over its meetings.
③ Where the chairperson cannot perform duties for unavoidable reasons, a member designated in advance by that chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf.
④ Regional Consultative Council members shall be appointed or commissioned by the head of the Si/Gun/Gu in consultation with the district superintendent of the relevant district office of education (where there is no district office of education, the institution prescribed by ordinance under the main clause of Article 12(1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply), from among persons with extensive knowledge and experience and a strong commitment to youth protection who fall under any of the following subparagraphs. Where there is no district superintendent, the head of the institution prescribed by ordinance under the main clause of Article 12(1) of the Act shall serve as district superintendent; hereinafter the same shall apply. <Amended 2014.6.11, 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
1. The bureau director responsible for youth protection in the relevant Si/Gun/Gu (the division director where the Si/Gun/Gu has no bureau director), and the bureau director responsible for student guidance in the district office of education (the division director where the district office has no bureau director);
2. A member of the relevant Si/Gun/Gu council;
3. A police official of the police station having jurisdiction over the relevant Si/Gun/Gu;
4. A teacher with at least five years of student guidance experience;
5. A judge, prosecutor or attorney-at-law;
6. A person who holds or has held a position of at least assistant professor at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act or an equivalent position at a youth-related research institution, and has expertise in school violence issues;
7. A person who has specialized in youth protection activities for at least five years at a youth guidance and protection organization;
8. A parent who serves or has served as a member of a school steering committee or Deliberation Committee;
9. Other persons with knowledge and experience in school violence prevention and youth protection.
⑤ Regional Consultative Council members shall serve a term of two years; provided that a member newly commissioned because of a Regional Committee member's resignation or similar circumstances shall serve the remainder of the predecessor's term.
⑥ The head of a Si/Gun/Gu may dismiss or discharge a Regional Consultative Council member under subparagraphs 2 through 9 of paragraph ④ where the member falls under any subparagraph of Article 3-2. <Added 2016.5.10>
⑦ There shall be one executive secretary to handle Regional Consultative Council affairs, selected jointly by its chairperson and the district superintendent from among officials of the Si/Gun/Gu or district office of education. <Amended 2014.6.11, 2016.5.10>

Article 8 (Composition of Dedicated Departments, Etc.)
① Under Article 11(1) of the Act, a division, officer's office or team shall be established in each City/Province office of education and district office of education to perform the following duties. <Amended 2014.6.11, 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
1. Establishment and promotion of measures to prevent and eradicate school violence;
2. Treatment of victim students of school violence and measures against aggressor students;
3. Measures necessary to restore relationships between victim students and aggressor students;
3-2. Legal support for victim students of school violence, including legal advice;
3-3. Investigation and counseling concerning school violence;
4. Other matters determined by the superintendent concerning prevention of and countermeasures against school violence and integrated support.
② Where necessary to perform school violence investigation and counseling duties under Article 11-2 of the Act efficiently, the superintendent may have the dedicated department under paragraph ① utilize experts in school violence investigation and counseling. <Added 2024.2.27>
③ Details concerning the roles, qualifications and payment of allowances for experts utilized under paragraph ② shall be determined by the superintendent. <Added 2024.2.27>

Article 9 (Fact-finding Surveys)
① A superintendent may, in consultation with the Minister of Education, jointly conduct surveys on school violence under Article 11(8) of the Act with other superintendents. <Amended 2013.3.23>
② A superintendent may entrust surveys on school violence to education-related research or survey institutions.

Article 10 (Establishment of Specialized Institutions, Etc.)
① Under Article 11(9) of the Act, the superintendent shall establish and operate specialized institutions in the City/Province office of education or district office of education to perform the following duties. <Amended 2014.6.11, 2024.2.27>
1. Investigation, counseling and related duties under Article 11-2(1) of the Act;
2. Operation of healing programs for victim students and aggressor students of school violence;
3. Operation of protective facilities for students' healing and recovery;
4. Integrated support, including legal support for victim students.
② The superintendent may entrust the operation of healing programs under subparagraph 2 of paragraph ① and protective facilities under subparagraph 3 of that paragraph to any of the following institutions, organizations or facilities. <Amended 2012.7.31, 2012.9.14, 2024.2.27>
1. Facilities operated by the State or local governments for youth protection, including youth shelters under subparagraph 1 of Article 31 of the Youth Welfare Support Act and youth protection and rehabilitation centers under Article 35(1) of the Youth Protection Act;
2. Youth activity facilities under Article 10 of the Youth Activity Promotion Act;
3. Youth-related organizations that prevent school violence and treat and educate victim students and aggressor students;
4. Hospitals with personnel specializing in youth psychotherapy;
5. Institutions, including religious institutions, operating programs for victim students and aggressor students of school violence and their parents;
6. Other institutions the superintendent considers suitable for operating healing programs.
③ Details concerning the establishment and operation of specialized institutions under paragraph ① shall be determined by the superintendent.

Article 11 (Entrustment of School Violence Investigation and Counseling Duties, Etc.)
Under Article 11-2(2) of the Act, the superintendent may entrust the duties under Article 11-2(1) of the Act to an institution or organization that has performed school violence prevention projects for at least three years and is recognized as suitable for performing school violence prevention and follow-up measures.
Article 12 (Cooperation with Relevant Institutions, Etc.)
Requests for cooperation concerning personal information and other information related to school violence under Article 11-3 of the Act shall be made in writing.

Article 13 (Establishment and Matters for Deliberation of Deliberation Committees)
① "Where grounds prescribed by Presidential Decree exist" in the proviso to Article 12(1) of the Act means where the victim student and aggressor student attend schools within the jurisdictions of different district offices of education. <Amended 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
② "Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in subparagraph 5 of Article 12(2) of the Act means matters proposed by a principal concerning the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence. <Amended 2020.2.25>

Article 14 (Composition and Operation of Deliberation Committees)
① Members of a Deliberation Committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the relevant district superintendent from among persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs. In such cases, a person falling under subparagraph 5-2 shall be included. <Amended 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
1. The bureau director or division director responsible for student guidance at the relevant district office of education (for an institution prescribed by ordinance under Article 12(1) of the Act, a public official or employee of that institution);
1-2. The bureau director or division director responsible for youth protection in the Si/Gun/Gu having jurisdiction over the jurisdictional area of the relevant district office of education;
2. A current or former teacher with at least two years of experience in school violence or student guidance duties;
2-2. A current or former educational specialist under Article 2(2) of the Educational Officials Act;
3. A parent under Article 13(1) of the Act;
4. A judge, prosecutor or attorney-at-law;
5. A police official of the police station having jurisdiction over the jurisdictional area of the relevant district office of education;
5-2. A police officer dedicated to school violence and related duties to prevent and eradicate school violence under Article 20-6(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "school police officer");
6. A person qualified as a medical doctor;
6-2. A person who holds or has held a position of at least assistant professor at a school under Article 2 of the Higher Education Act or an equivalent position at a youth-related research institution, and has expertise in school violence issues;
6-3. A person who has specialized in youth protection activities at a youth guidance and protection organization for at least two years;
7. Other persons with extensive knowledge and experience in school violence prevention and youth protection.
② The chairperson of a Deliberation Committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the district superintendent from among its members. Where the chairperson cannot perform duties for unavoidable reasons, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall act on the chairperson's behalf. <Amended 2020.2.25>
③ Deliberation Committee members shall serve a term of two years; provided that a member newly commissioned because of a member's resignation or similar circumstances shall serve the remainder of the predecessor's term. <Amended 2020.2.25>
④ The district superintendent may dismiss or discharge a Deliberation Committee member under subparagraphs 2, 2-2, 3 through 5, 5-2, 6, 6-2, 6-3 and 7 of paragraph ① where the member falls under any subparagraph of Article 3-2. <Added 2016.5.10, 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
⑤ Deliberation Committee meetings shall open with a majority of members in office present and adopt resolutions with the approval of a majority of members present. <Amended 2016.5.10, 2020.2.25>
⑥ The chairperson shall designate one executive secretary to handle the affairs of the Deliberation Committee from among public officials of the relevant district office of education (including employees in the case of an institution prescribed by ordinance under Article 12(1) of the Act). <Amended 2016.5.10, 2020.2.25>
⑦ Members attending Deliberation Committee meetings may be paid allowances and travel expenses within budgetary limits; provided that this shall not apply where a member who is a public official attends a meeting directly related to the member's official duties. <Amended 2016.5.10, 2020.2.25>
⑧ Where necessary, a Deliberation Committee may have teachers of the school where the school violence occurred or experts in fields related to the prevention of and countermeasures against school violence attend, or hear their opinions in writing or by other means. <Amended 2020.2.25>
⑨ Matters necessary for the operation of Deliberation Committees in addition to those provided for in paragraphs ① through ⑧ shall be determined by the district superintendent. <Added 2020.2.25>

Article 14-2 (Subcommittees)
① A subcommittee may be established within a Deliberation Committee where necessary for the efficient performance of its duties.
② A subcommittee under paragraph ① (hereinafter referred to as a "subcommittee") shall consist of members of the Deliberation Committee.
③ A Deliberation Committee may, where necessary, delegate matters for deliberation to a subcommittee. In such cases, matters deliberated on and resolved by the subcommittee shall be deemed deliberated on and resolved by the Deliberation Committee.
④ Upon completing deliberation, a subcommittee shall report its results to the Deliberation Committee.
⑤ Matters necessary for the establishment and operation of subcommittees in addition to those provided for in paragraphs ① through ④ shall be determined by the district superintendent.

Article 14-3 (Internal Resolution by Principals)
Where a principal resolves a school violence case internally under Article 13-2(1) of the Act, the principal shall endeavor to prevent further school violence between the victim student and aggressor student and may, where necessary, operate a program to restore relationships among the victim student, aggressor student and their guardians.

Article 15 (Establishment of Counseling Rooms)
A counseling room under Article 14(1) of the Act shall be established in a location convenient for counseling activities and equipped with the following facilities and equipment.
1. Facilities and equipment necessary for counseling, including internet access facilities and telephones;
2. Partitions and soundproofing facilities to protect the privacy of persons receiving counseling.

Article 16 (Operation of Dedicated Bodies, Etc.)
① Parents serving as members of a body dedicated to school violence issues under Article 14(3) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "dedicated body") shall be commissioned by the principal from among persons recommended by the school steering committee under Article 31 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act; provided that, for a school without a school steering committee, the principal shall commission them.
② A dedicated body shall report to the principal its findings as to whether perpetration and harm occurred.
③ Matters necessary for the operation of dedicated bodies in addition to those provided for in paragraphs ① and ② shall be determined by the principal.

Article 17 (School Violence Prevention Education)
① Education under Article 11(13) of the Act shall include the following matters. <Added 2024.2.27>
1. Requirements and procedures for internal resolution by principals under Article 13-2 of the Act;
2. Separation of perpetrators (including teachers; hereinafter the same shall apply in Article 17-2) from victim students, and emergency protection measures for victim students, under the proviso to the introductory part of Article 16(1) of the Act;
3. Measures against aggressor students under Article 17(5) and (6) of the Act;
4. Other roles of principals and vice-principals necessary for school violence prevention and response.
② Under Article 15(7) of the Act, principals shall provide school violence prevention education for students, school personnel and parents in accordance with the following criteria. <Amended 2024.2.27>
1. Education shall be provided at least once each semester. Details, including frequency, hours and instructors, shall be determined by the principal according to the school's circumstances.
2. School violence prevention education for students shall, in principle, be provided by class, but may be provided simultaneously to all students in one location according to the school's circumstances.
3. Students, school personnel and parents shall, in principle, be educated separately, but may be educated together depending on the content.
4. Various methods, including lectures, discussions and role-playing, shall be used, and diverse materials or programs shall be utilized.
5. School violence prevention education for school personnel shall include school violence statutes, responses to school violence incidents and methods of operating school violence prevention programs for students.
6. School violence prevention education for parents shall include identification of signs of school violence, responses to school violence incidents and character education at home.

Article 17-2 (Measures to Separate Perpetrators and Victim Students)
① "Special circumstances prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as the victim student's opposition" in the proviso to the introductory part of Article 16(1) of the Act means any of the following cases. <Amended 2024.2.27, 2025.5.20>
1. The victim student expresses opposition;
2. The perpetrator or victim student is not engaged in educational activities under subparagraph 4 of Article 2 of the Act on the Prevention of and Compensation for Accidents at School;
3. The case constitutes minor school violence meeting all the conditions under the subparagraphs of Article 13-2(1) of the Act;
4. The perpetrator and victim student have already been separated by measures under the former sentences of Article 17(5) and (6) of the Act.
② Matters necessary for the period and methods of separating perpetrators and victim students under the proviso to the introductory part of Article 16(1) of the Act shall be determined by the Minister of Education. <Added 2025.5.20>

Article 18 (Scope of Support for Victim Students, Etc.)
① The scope of support for victim students borne by a School Safety Mutual Aid Association or City/Province office of education under the proviso to Article 16(6) of the Act shall be as follows. <Amended 2021.6.22>
1. Expenses of receiving psychological counseling and advice at a specialized psychological counseling institution designated by the superintendent;
2. Expenses of receiving temporary protection at an institution designated by the superintendent;
3. Expenses of receiving treatment, recuperation for treatment or pharmaceuticals at medical institutions established under the Medical Service Act; public health centers, public health and medical centers, and branch public health centers established under the Regional Public Health Act; primary health care posts established under the Act on Special Measures for Public Health and Medical Services in Agricultural and Fishing Villages, Etc.; pharmacies registered under the Pharmaceutical Affairs Act; and the Korea Orphan and Essential Drug Center established under Article 91 of that Act.
② Article 41 of the Act on the Prevention of and Compensation for Accidents at School shall apply mutatis mutandis to the procedures for victim students and guardians seeking support for expenses under paragraph ① to claim expenses from a School Safety Mutual Aid Association or City/Province office of education, and to procedures for those entities to pay such expenses.
③ The scope of reimbursement claims made by a School Safety Mutual Aid Association or City/Province office of education against an aggressor student's guardian under Article 16(6) of the Act shall encompass all expenses paid to the victim student under paragraph ②. <Amended 2021.9.29>

Article 18-2 (Designation and Operation of Victim Student Support Assistants)
① Under Article 16-3 of the Act, a superintendent or district superintendent may designate a person who satisfies all the following requirements and has extensive knowledge and experience in youth protection and emotional support as an assistant connecting victim students with services and support institutions for legal assistance, counseling, protection and other needs (hereinafter referred to as a "victim student support assistant").
1. The person shall fall under any of the following items.
(a) A social worker under Article 11 of the Social Welfare Services Act;
(b) A current or former teacher;
(c) A current or former police official;
(d) Another person recognized by the superintendent or district superintendent as having extensive knowledge and experience in youth protection, emotional support and related matters.
2. The person shall not fall under any of the following items.
(a) A person falling under any subparagraph of Article 33 of the State Public Officials Act;
(b) A person who committed a sexual offense against a child or youth under the Act on the Protection of Children and Youth against Sexual Abuse or a sexual crime under the Act on Special Cases concerning the Punishment, Etc. of Sexual Crimes, and for whom ten years have not elapsed since the date a sentence of a fine became final, or since the date execution of a sentence of imprisonment without labor or heavier punishment, or medical treatment and custody, was completed, suspended or remitted;
(c) An owner or worker of an establishment prohibited from admitting or employing juveniles under subitem 3 and subitems 7 through 9 of item (a) of subparagraph 5 of Article 2 of the Youth Protection Act.
② A person wishing to serve as a victim student support assistant shall submit to the superintendent or district superintendent a written confirmation that the person does not fall under any item of subparagraph 2 of paragraph ①.
③ The superintendent or district superintendent may withdraw the designation of a victim student support assistant under paragraph ① in any of the following cases.
1. The person becomes unable to perform duties because of physical or mental weakness;
2. The person engages in misconduct related to duties;
3. The person is deemed unsuitable as a victim student support assistant because of neglect of duties, damage to dignity or other grounds;
4. The person expresses difficulty in performing duties as a victim student support assistant;
5. The person is found to fall under any item of subparagraph 2 of paragraph ①.
④ Matters necessary for the operation of victim student support assistants in addition to those provided for in paragraphs ① through ③ shall be determined by the superintendent or district superintendent.

Article 18-3 (Content and Methods of Support for Victim Students of Cyberviolence, Etc.)
① Under Article 16-4(1) of the Act, the Minister of Education may provide the following support to students harmed by the distribution of visual recordings, audio recordings, copies, edited materials, personal information, false statements and similar materials constituting cyberviolence (hereinafter referred to in this Article as "recordings, etc.").
1. Counseling on harm and related matters requiring deletion of recordings, etc.;
2. Collection of information concerning harm caused by distribution of recordings, etc.;
3. Verification and inspection of whether recordings, etc. have been deleted;
4. Other matters the Minister of Education considers necessary concerning deletion support for recordings, etc.
② A victim student under paragraph ①, the student's guardian, or a representative designated by the victim student or guardian (hereinafter referred to in this Article as an "applicant for deletion support") may request the support under the subparagraphs of paragraph ① from the Minister of Education, with the following documents.
1. Documents proving the identity of the applicant for deletion support;
2. Documents proving the relationship with the victim student (only where the applicant is the victim student's guardian or a representative designated by that guardian);
3. A power of attorney bearing the handwritten signature of the victim student or guardian, and a copy of a document proving the victim student's identity (only where the applicant is a representative designated by the victim student or guardian).
③ The Minister of Education may request necessary cooperation from relevant institutions or organizations, including central administrative agencies, concerning the support under the subparagraphs of paragraph ①.
④ Where the Minister of Education intends to exercise a right to reimbursement under Article 16-4(4) of the Act, the Minister shall notify the aggressor student responsible for cyberviolence or the student's guardian in writing to pay, specifying the basis for calculating the amount claimed and other relevant matters. In such cases, the payment deadline shall be within 60 days from the notification date.

Article 19 (Criteria for Applying Measures against Aggressor Students)
The criteria for applying individual measures under Article 17(1) of the Act shall be determined taking the following matters into account, and detailed criteria shall be determined and publicly notified by the Minister of Education. <Amended 2013.3.23>
1. The severity, persistence and intentional nature of the school violence committed by the aggressor student;
2. The aggressor student's degree of remorse;
3. The prospect of guiding the aggressor student through the relevant measure;
4. The degree of reconciliation between the aggressor student and guardian and the victim student and guardian;
5. Whether the victim student is a student with a disability.

Article 20 (Measures to Transfer Aggressor Students)
① Where a Deliberation Committee requests the transfer of an aggressor student under Article 17(1) of the Act, the district superintendent shall notify the principal of the student's school. Upon receiving such notification, the principal shall request the superintendent or district superintendent to assign a school for the student to transfer to without delay. <Amended 2020.2.25>
② When assigning a school for an aggressor student to transfer to, the superintendent or district superintendent shall consider a sufficient distance to protect the victim student and other relevant factors. Where assigning a school outside the jurisdiction, the relevant superintendent or district superintendent shall be notified.
③ A superintendent or district superintendent notified under paragraph ② shall assign a school for the aggressor student to transfer to.
④ When an aggressor student transferred under paragraphs ② and ③ and the victim student advance to schools at the next level, the superintendent or district superintendent shall assign them to different schools. In such cases, the school to be entered by the victim student shall be assigned first.

Article 21 (Prior Suspension of Attendance of Aggressor Students, Etc.)
① A principal may impose suspension of attendance or a change of class under the former sentences of Article 17(5) and (6) of the Act in any of the following cases. <Amended 2024.2.27>
1. Two or more students intentionally and persistently commit violence;
2. School violence causes an injury requiring at least two weeks of treatment;
3. Violence is committed in retaliation for reporting school violence, giving statements, providing materials or similar conduct;
4. The principal determines that the victim student urgently needs protection from the aggressor student;
5. The victim student and the student's guardian request separation from the aggressor student.
② When intending to impose suspension of attendance or a change of class under paragraph ①, the principal shall hear the opinions of the student concerned or the student's guardian; provided that this shall not apply where the principal has attempted to hear their opinions but they have not complied. <Amended 2024.2.27>

Article 22 (Additional Measures for Aggressor Students' Refusal or Evasion of Measures)
① Within seven days after receiving notification from the district superintendent that a student subjected to any of the measures under subparagraphs 2 through 9 of Article 17(1) of the Act has refused or evaded the measure, a Deliberation Committee may, under Article 17(15) of the Act, request that the district superintendent take other additional measures. <Amended 2020.2.25, 2024.2.27>
② A superintendent shall investigate a district superintendent or principal under Article 17(16) of the Act in the following cases. In such cases, the investigation shall, in principle, be conducted in writing, but may be conducted face to face where necessary. <Added 2024.2.27>
1. Where a Deliberation Committee has requested measures from a district superintendent under Article 17(1) of the Act, but the district superintendent has not taken those measures within 14 days under paragraph (9) of that Article;
2. Where a principal has taken measures under Article 17(4) through (6) of the Act but the aggressor student has refused or evaded them, and the principal has not imposed discipline under paragraph (10) of that Article;
3. Where a Deliberation Committee has requested additional different measures from a district superintendent under Article 17(15) of the Act, but the district superintendent has delayed or failed to implement them.
Article 23 (Readmission of Expelled Students, Etc.)
① Under Article 17(17) of the Act, the superintendent shall comprehensively consider the degree to which a student expelled under subparagraph 9 of Article 17(1) of the Act has been guided, the student's educational potential and other relevant matters, and devise measures suitable for the student's sound development, including admission to an alternative school under Article 60-3 of the Elementary and Secondary Education Act. <Amended 2024.2.27>
② Detailed matters necessary for measures against aggressor students and readmission in addition to those provided for in paragraph ① shall be determined by the superintendent.

Article 24 (Guarantee of Victim Students' Right to Make Statements)
① An administrative appeals commission may dispense with hearing opinions under the proviso to Article 17-4(1) of the Act in the following cases.
1. The victim student or the student's guardian clearly indicates a waiver of the opportunity to state opinions;
2. The victim student or the student's guardian has already stated opinions sufficiently on the case, and a further statement is deemed unnecessary;
3. The administrative appeals commission otherwise considers hearing the opinions of the victim student or guardian manifestly difficult.
② When hearing the opinions of a victim student or the student's guardian under the main clause of Article 17-4(1) of the Act, the administrative appeals commission shall give an opportunity to state opinions on the extent and consequences of the harm, measures against the aggressor student, and other matters concerning the case.
③ Where the administrative appeals commission considers that the victim student or guardian cannot make a sufficient statement when facing the aggressor student or guardian, it may have the statement made after asking the aggressor student or guardian to leave, or have the victim student or guardian submit opinions in writing.

Article 25 (Application for Dispute Mediation)
Either side among the victim student, aggressor student or their guardians (hereinafter referred to as the "parties to the dispute") may apply for dispute mediation under Article 18 of the Act to the Deliberation Committee or superintendent authorized to mediate the dispute concerned, by a document stating the following matters. <Amended 2020.2.25>
1. The name and address of the applicant for dispute mediation;
2. The guardian's name and address;
3. Grounds for the application for dispute mediation.

Article 26 (Exclusion, Challenge and Recusal of Deliberation Committee Members)
① When requesting measures concerning victim students and aggressor students or mediating disputes under Articles 16, 17 and 18 of the Act, a Deliberation Committee member shall be excluded from the case in any of the following circumstances. <Amended 2020.2.25>
1. The member, the member's spouse or former spouse is or has been a guardian of the victim student or aggressor student in the case;
2. The member is or has been a relative of the victim student or aggressor student in the case;
3. It is otherwise recognized that the member has a personal acquaintance or connection with the victim student or aggressor student in the case.
② Where a Deliberation Committee meets concerning school violence or a dispute arises and substantial grounds exist for considering that a committee member cannot be expected to deliberate impartially, a party to the dispute may explain the facts in writing to the Deliberation Committee and apply to challenge that member. <Amended 2020.2.25>
③ Upon receiving a challenge application under paragraph ②, the Deliberation Committee shall decide by resolution whether to disqualify the member. In such cases, the member subject to the application shall not participate in that resolution. <Amended 2020.2.25>
④ A Deliberation Committee member falling under the grounds in paragraph ① or ② may voluntarily recuse from the case. <Amended 2020.2.25>

Article 27 (Commencement of Dispute Mediation)
① A Deliberation Committee or superintendent receiving an application for dispute mediation under Article 25 shall begin mediation within five days after receiving it. <Amended 2020.2.25>
② The Deliberation Committee or superintendent shall notify the parties to the dispute of the date, time and place of mediation. <Amended 2020.2.25>
③ Where either party notified under paragraph ② cannot attend for unavoidable reasons, that party may request the Deliberation Committee or superintendent to postpone mediation. In such cases, the Deliberation Committee or superintendent shall set a new mediation date. <Amended 2020.2.25>
④ The Deliberation Committee or superintendent may designate a person responsible for dispute mediation from among members of the Deliberation Committee or Regional Committee, or consult an external specialized institution on matters concerning the dispute. <Amended 2020.2.25>

Article 28 (Refusal, Suspension and Termination of Dispute Mediation)
① A Deliberation Committee or superintendent may refuse to commence or suspend dispute mediation on any of the following grounds. <Amended 2020.2.25>
1. Either party refuses dispute mediation;
2. A criminal complaint or accusation has been filed against the aggressor student, or civil litigation has been instituted, in relation to school violence involving the victim student or other persons;
3. The application for mediation is manifestly false or deemed to lack justifiable grounds.
② A Deliberation Committee or superintendent shall terminate dispute mediation on any of the following grounds. <Amended 2020.2.25>
1. Mediation is successful, including where the parties reach agreement or accept the mediation proposal presented by the Deliberation Committee or superintendent;
2. Mediation has not succeeded after one month from its commencement.
③ Where a Deliberation Committee or superintendent refuses to commence or suspends mediation under paragraph ①, or terminates it under subparagraph 2 of paragraph ②, the reasons shall be notified to each party. <Amended 2020.2.25>

Article 29 (Handling Results of Dispute Mediation)
① Where mediation succeeds, the Deliberation Committee or superintendent shall prepare a written agreement stating the following matters and notify each party to the dispute and the principals of the schools attended by the victim and aggressor students. <Amended 2020.2.25>
1. The addresses and names of the parties to the dispute;
2. The content of the dispute mediated:
(a) The background of the dispute;
(b) The issues in mediation, including the parties' opinions;
3. The results of mediation.
② The written agreement under paragraph ① shall be signed and sealed by the parties and the members participating in mediation where a Deliberation Committee mediated, or by the parties and superintendent where the superintendent mediated. <Amended 2020.2.25>
③ The chairperson of the Deliberation Committee shall report the results of dispute mediation to the superintendent. <Amended 2020.2.25>

Article 30 (Establishment and Operation of Emergency Telephone Lines)
Emergency telephone lines under Article 20-2 of the Act shall be established in school violence-related bodies operated by the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency and Commissioners of City/Province Police Agencies. <Amended 2020.12.31>

Article 31 (Use of Information and Communications Networks, Etc.)
Under Article 20-4(3) of the Act, the State, local governments or superintendents may entrust school violence prevention duties using information and communications networks to the following institutions and organizations.
1. The Korea Education and Research Information Service established under the Korea Education and Research Information Service Act;
2. Enterprises with a record of conducting educational projects using information and communications networks under entrustment from public institutions;
3. Institutions or organizations that have performed school violence prevention projects for at least three years.

Article 31-2 (Operation of School Police Officers)
① When assigning school police officers under Article 20-6(1) of the Act, the Commissioner General of the Korean National Police Agency shall consider expertise necessary for school violence duties, including possession of degrees or qualifications related to student counseling and experience in student guidance. <Amended 2024.2.27>
② School police officers shall perform the following duties.
1. School violence prevention activities;
2. Protection of victim students and guidance of aggressor students;
3. Collection of information on school violence groups;
4. Prevention of the formation of and disbandment of school violence groups;
5. Other duties the Commissioner General, in consultation with the Minister of Education, considers necessary for the prevention and eradication of school violence.
③ The head of the police agency to which a school police officer belongs and the principal shall cooperate with each other to prevent and eradicate school violence.

Article 32 (Integrated Monitoring of Fixed Video Information Processing Devices)
The State and local governments intending to integrate and monitor fixed video information processing devices under Article 20-7(1) of the Act shall collect the opinions of relevant experts and interested parties through the following procedures. <Amended 2018.12.31, 2023.9.12>
1. Administrative pre-announcement or hearing of opinions under the Administrative Procedures Act;
2. Deliberation by the school steering committee.

Article 33 (Scope of Confidential Information)
The scope of confidential information under Article 21(1) of the Act shall be as follows.
1. Personal information, including names, resident registration numbers and addresses, of victim students and aggressor students of school violence and their families;
2. Individual statements concerning deliberations and resolutions on victim students and aggressor students of school violence;
3. Other matters whose external disclosure would clearly risk causing controversy between the parties to the dispute.

Article 33-2 (Processing of Unique Identifying Information)
① Where unavoidable for performing the following duties, the State, local governments or principals may process materials containing resident registration numbers or alien registration numbers under Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act.
1. Duties concerning verification of whether student protection personnel are disqualified under Article 20-5(2) of the Act;
2. Duties concerning criminal history checks on student protection personnel under Article 20-5(5) of the Act.
② A specialized institution or organization entrusted with the deployment and utilization of student protection personnel under Article 20-5(3) of the Act may, where unavoidable for performing the following duties, process materials containing resident registration numbers or alien registration numbers under Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act.
1. Duties concerning verification of whether student protection personnel are disqualified under Article 20-5(2) of the Act;
2. Duties concerning applications for criminal history checks on student protection personnel under Article 20-5(6) of the Act.

